{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018r8z8ELTxzmzKM5TF9zjBi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Tiuv7BknzVCPhTpc13ZXwG","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Acceptance rate ~39%\n- Rating scale: 1 (strong reject) to 5 (strong accept), borderline = 3\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality/Soundness, Clarity, Significance, Relevance to Robot Learning\n- Papers must demonstrate relevance to Robot Learning through either explicitly addressing a learning question for physical robots or testing on physical robots\n- Papers must include a limitations section. Honestly reported limitations should be treated kindly.\n\n## Your Review Focus\nYou are responsible for evaluating:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls adequate? Are confounds addressed?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the statistical methods appropriate? Is the n=3 seed design sufficient? Are effect sizes properly reported?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: Are all claimed conclusions supported by experiments? Are there missing experiments?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n5. **Evaluation metrics**: Are CAR and TSR well-defined and appropriate?\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make Vision-Language-Action Models Safer? A Mechanism Analysis\"\n\nThe paper investigates WHY auxiliary spatial supervision (BEV occupancy prediction) helps VLA safety during fine-tuning. Through 5 gradient-flow conditions (Full OCC, Detach, Random OCC, Vanilla, Detach+LayerNorm), the paper argues:\n- Gradient flow through the encoder is necessary and sufficient for maintaining collision avoidance\n- Spatial label content is secondary\n- Protection is binary (step-function), not dose-dependent\n- The mechanism is: gradient flow → gradient alignment → representation stability → maintained CAR\n\nKey experimental results:\n- Table 1: Core ablation on LIBERO-Spatial (3 seeds, 50K steps). Detach CAR=0.746 vs Vanilla 0.780 (-3.4pp, d=3.9). Full OCC 0.797, Random OCC 0.789.\n- Table 2-3: Gradient cosine analysis. Full OCC cosine=0.18 vs Detach 0.02, strongest in spatial layers L9-18 (0.24).\n- Table 4-5: CKA analysis. Detach diverges from Vanilla especially in L9-18 (CKA=0.80 vs Full OCC 0.95).\n- Table 6: Dose-response. 15K-50K cutoff all give CAR 0.775-0.797 (plateau), only 0K drops to 0.746.\n- Table 7-8: 2×2 temporal design. Any GF (early or late) protects (p&lt;0.01), no interaction.\n- Table 9: Detach+LN (0.748) ≈ Detach+BN (0.746), ruling out BatchNorm artifact.\n- Table 10-11: LIBERO-Object replication. All 4 patterns replicate consistently.\n\nArchitecture: DINOv2 (1024-dim) + SigLIP (1152-dim) → concat (2176-dim) → action head. LoRA rank=32, α=16, scaling=0.5, 50K steps, lr=5e-4. OccHead: 1.2M params (0.016% of 7.65B model). Focal loss with exponential decay w(t)=0.01·exp(-t/50000). SafeLIBERO benchmark with contact-based collision detection. 50 rollouts/task × 10 tasks = 500 episodes.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes these imagined experiments:\n1. Gradient Cosine &amp; Norm Analysis (plan_023) — COVERED in paper\n2. CKA Representation Analysis (plan_024) — COVERED in paper\n3. Cutoff@15K (plan_020) — COVERED in paper\n4. Cutoff@25K (plan_021/026) — COVERED in paper\n5. Cutoff@45K (plan_022) — COVERED in paper\n6. Reverse Cutoff 35K-50K (plan_025) — COVERED in paper\n7. Detach+LayerNorm (plan_027) — COVERED in paper\n8. LIBERO-Object replication (plan_028) — COVERED in paper\n9. Cutoff@15K additional seed (plan_029) — Mentioned briefly (\"seed 2 yields CAR=0.772\")\n10. Cutoff@45K additional seed (plan_030) — Mentioned briefly (\"seed 2 yields CAR=0.788\")\n\nCoverage appears comprehensive — all P0/P1 experiments are in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a structured review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Scores**: Overall (1-5), Soundness (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Significance (1-4), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider what a top-tier robotics/ML reviewer would flag."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":291,"cache_read_input_tokens":65348,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":291},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fd64fbd-040e-43dd-8257-d68950227536","uuid":"d49e2da5-9d38-481c-a290-a68a445d23af"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"abc9a6914c5807e71","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tiuv7BknzVCPhTpc13ZXwG","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Acceptance rate ~39%\n- Rating scale: 1 (strong reject) to 5 (strong accept), borderline = 3\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality/Soundness, Clarity, Significance, Relevance to Robot Learning\n- Papers must demonstrate relevance to Robot Learning through either explicitly addressing a learning question for physical robots or testing on physical robots\n- Papers must include a limitations section. Honestly reported limitations should be treated kindly.\n\n## Your Review Focus\nYou are responsible for evaluating:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls adequate? Are confounds addressed?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the statistical methods appropriate? Is the n=3 seed design sufficient? Are effect sizes properly reported?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: Are all claimed conclusions supported by experiments? Are there missing experiments?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n5. **Evaluation metrics**: Are CAR and TSR well-defined and appropriate?\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make Vision-Language-Action Models Safer? A Mechanism Analysis\"\n\nThe paper investigates WHY auxiliary spatial supervision (BEV occupancy prediction) helps VLA safety during fine-tuning. Through 5 gradient-flow conditions (Full OCC, Detach, Random OCC, Vanilla, Detach+LayerNorm), the paper argues:\n- Gradient flow through the encoder is necessary and sufficient for maintaining collision avoidance\n- Spatial label content is secondary\n- Protection is binary (step-function), not dose-dependent\n- The mechanism is: gradient flow → gradient alignment → representation stability → maintained CAR\n\nKey experimental results:\n- Table 1: Core ablation on LIBERO-Spatial (3 seeds, 50K steps). Detach CAR=0.746 vs Vanilla 0.780 (-3.4pp, d=3.9). Full OCC 0.797, Random OCC 0.789.\n- Table 2-3: Gradient cosine analysis. Full OCC cosine=0.18 vs Detach 0.02, strongest in spatial layers L9-18 (0.24).\n- Table 4-5: CKA analysis. Detach diverges from Vanilla especially in L9-18 (CKA=0.80 vs Full OCC 0.95).\n- Table 6: Dose-response. 15K-50K cutoff all give CAR 0.775-0.797 (plateau), only 0K drops to 0.746.\n- Table 7-8: 2×2 temporal design. Any GF (early or late) protects (p&lt;0.01), no interaction.\n- Table 9: Detach+LN (0.748) ≈ Detach+BN (0.746), ruling out BatchNorm artifact.\n- Table 10-11: LIBERO-Object replication. All 4 patterns replicate consistently.\n\nArchitecture: DINOv2 (1024-dim) + SigLIP (1152-dim) → concat (2176-dim) → action head. LoRA rank=32, α=16, scaling=0.5, 50K steps, lr=5e-4. OccHead: 1.2M params (0.016% of 7.65B model). Focal loss with exponential decay w(t)=0.01·exp(-t/50000). SafeLIBERO benchmark with contact-based collision detection. 50 rollouts/task × 10 tasks = 500 episodes.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes these imagined experiments:\n1. Gradient Cosine &amp; Norm Analysis (plan_023) — COVERED in paper\n2. CKA Representation Analysis (plan_024) — COVERED in paper\n3. Cutoff@15K (plan_020) — COVERED in paper\n4. Cutoff@25K (plan_021/026) — COVERED in paper\n5. Cutoff@45K (plan_022) — COVERED in paper\n6. Reverse Cutoff 35K-50K (plan_025) — COVERED in paper\n7. Detach+LayerNorm (plan_027) — COVERED in paper\n8. LIBERO-Object replication (plan_028) — COVERED in paper\n9. Cutoff@15K additional seed (plan_029) — Mentioned briefly (\"seed 2 yields CAR=0.772\")\n10. Cutoff@45K additional seed (plan_030) — Mentioned briefly (\"seed 2 yields CAR=0.788\")\n\nCoverage appears comprehensive — all P0/P1 experiments are in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a structured review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Scores**: Overall (1-5), Soundness (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Significance (1-4), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider what a top-tier robotics/ML reviewer would flag.","uuid":"d87e815c-52a5-4eda-8e48-3234f36c3b22","session_id":"6fd64fbd-040e-43dd-8257-d68950227536"}
