{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Wn5wBK9Zr4kPM3koy6YCSL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BQyhFNnWWFELz78F9TTfJd","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/occsafe_vla/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T034027/best/paper.tex","offset":200,"limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9149,"cache_read_input_tokens":60999,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9149,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tiuv7BknzVCPhTpc13ZXwG","session_id":"6fd64fbd-040e-43dd-8257-d68950227536","uuid":"9c4b65aa-5f09-4b94-9a90-e246dbae7fe4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BQyhFNnWWFELz78F9TTfJd","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n201\t\\toprule\n202\tCondition & Early (L1--8) & Mid-Spatial (L9--18) & Late (L19--24) & Mean \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\tFull OCC   & $0.97 \\pm 0.01$ & $\\mathbf{0.95} \\pm 0.02$ & $0.96 \\pm 0.01$ & $0.96 \\pm 0.01$ \\\\\n205\tDetach     & $0.90 \\pm 0.03$ & $0.80 \\pm 0.04$          & $0.86 \\pm 0.03$ & $0.85 \\pm 0.02$ \\\\\n206\tRandom OCC & $0.94 \\pm 0.02$ & $0.91 \\pm 0.03$          & $0.92 \\pm 0.02$ & $0.92 \\pm 0.02$ \\\\\n207\t\\bottomrule\n208\t\\end{tabular}\n209\t\\end{table}\n210\t\n211\t\\begin{table}[h]\n212\t\\centering\n213\t\\caption{Cross-condition CKA matrix for spatial layers L9--18 (3-seed mean).}\n214\t\\label{tab:cka-cross}\n215\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n216\t\\toprule\n217\t & Full OCC & Detach & Vanilla & Random OCC \\\\\n218\t\\midrule\n219\tFull OCC   & 1.00 & 0.78 & 0.95 & 0.90 \\\\\n220\tDetach     & 0.78 & 1.00 & 0.80 & 0.82 \\\\\n221\tVanilla    & 0.95 & 0.80 & 1.00 & 0.91 \\\\\n222\tRandom OCC & 0.90 & 0.82 & 0.91 & 1.00 \\\\\n223\t\\bottomrule\n224\t\\end{tabular}\n225\t\\end{table}\n226\t\n227\tGradient flow acts as a \\emph{representation stabilizer}. Full OCC maintains near-identical representations to Vanilla (CKA $= 0.95$ in spatial layers), meaning that occupancy gradient flow does not distort the encoder but rather prevents drift. Detach shows systematic representation divergence concentrated in the mid-spatial layers (CKA $= 0.80$), forming a characteristic V-shaped dip---precisely the layers where DINOv2 encodes spatial geometry. This divergence is consistent with the per-task analysis: tasks requiring spatial reasoning (T3, T5, T6) show the largest Detach degradation.\n228\t\n229\tThe causal chain emerges: gradient flow $\\to$ gradient alignment (Sec.~\\ref{sec:exp-gradient}) $\\to$ representation stability (this section) $\\to$ preserved spatial reasoning $\\to$ maintained CAR.\n230\t\n231\t%--------------------------------------------------\n232\t\\subsection{Dose-Response: Gradient Flow Duration}\n233\t\\label{sec:exp-dose}\n234\t%--------------------------------------------------\n235\t\n236\tIf gradient flow is the protective mechanism, how much is needed? We vary the duration of occupancy gradient flow by cutting off the auxiliary loss at different training steps.\n237\t\n238\t\\begin{table}[h]\n239\t\\centering\n240\t\\caption{Dose-response: CAR as a function of gradient flow duration (500 episodes per condition).}\n241\t\\label{tab:dose}\n242\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n243\t\\toprule\n244\tCutoff Step & \\% Training & GF Duration & CAR & TSR & Protected? \\\\\n245\t\\midrule\n246\t0K (Detach) & 0\\%  & 0 steps  & 0.746 & 0.812 & No  \\\\\n247\t15K         & 30\\% & 15K steps & 0.775 & 0.895 & Yes \\\\\n248\t25K         & 50\\% & 25K steps & 0.785 & 0.908 & Yes \\\\\n249\t35K         & 70\\% & 35K steps & 0.785 & 0.913 & Yes \\\\\n250\t45K         & 90\\% & 45K steps & 0.792 & 0.918 & Yes \\\\\n251\t50K (Full)  & 100\\% & 50K steps & 0.797 & 0.894 & Yes \\\\\n252\t\\bottomrule\n253\t\\end{tabular}\n254\t\\end{table}\n255\t\n256\tThe dose-response curve exhibits a striking \\emph{step-function} pattern. All cutoff points from 15K onward produce CAR in the range $[0.775, 0.797]$, a spread of only 2.2\\,pp that falls within the Vanilla 95\\% CI ($0.780 \\pm 0.024$). Only at 0K (Detach) does CAR drop sharply to 0.746, a $-3.4$\\,pp cliff.\n257\t\n258\tThis binary protection pattern has two implications. First, it rules out a dose-dependent alternative: more gradient flow does not produce proportionally more protection. Second, it establishes a practical upper bound on the required duration: 15K steps (30\\% of training) provides full protection, and the actual threshold may be even lower.\n259\t\n260\tAdditional seeds for the 15K and 45K cutoff points confirm robustness: Cutoff@15K seed 2 yields CAR $= 0.772$, and Cutoff@45K seed 2 yields CAR $= 0.788$, both consistent with the primary runs.\n261\t\n262\t%--------------------------------------------------\n263\t\\subsection{Temporal Design: Early vs.\\ Late Gradient Flow}\n264\t\\label{sec:exp-temporal}\n265\t%--------------------------------------------------\n266\t\n267\tDoes gradient flow protection require early exposure, or can it be introduced at any point? We construct a $2 \\times 2$ temporal design by combining the Cutoff@35K (early-only: gradient flow during steps 0--35K) with a \\emph{reverse cutoff} condition (late-only: gradient flow during steps 35K--50K).\n268\t\n269\t\\begin{table}[h]\n270\t\\centering\n271\t\\caption{$2 \\times 2$ temporal design: early vs.\\ late gradient flow.}\n272\t\\label{tab:temporal}\n273\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccl}\n274\t\\toprule\n275\tCondition & GF 0--35K & GF 35K--50K & CAR & TSR & Protected? \\\\\n276\t\\midrule\n277\tFull OCC       & Yes & Yes & 0.797 & 0.894 & Yes \\\\\n278\tCutoff@35K     & Yes & No  & 0.785 & 0.913 & Yes \\\\\n279\tReverse Cutoff & No  & Yes & 0.778 & 0.898 & Yes \\\\\n280\tDetach         & No  & No  & 0.746 & 0.812 & No  \\\\\n281\t\\bottomrule\n282\t\\end{tabular}\n283\t\\end{table}\n284\t\n285\t\\begin{table}[h]\n286\t\\centering\n287\t\\caption{$2 \\times 2$ factorial analysis of temporal gradient flow effects.}\n288\t\\label{tab:factorial}\n289\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n290\t\\toprule\n291\tFactor & CAR Effect & $p$-value & Interpretation \\\\\n292\t\\midrule\n293\tEarly GF (main)        & $+0.012$ & 0.19    & Not significant alone \\\\\n294\tLate GF (main)         & $+0.010$ & 0.24    & Not significant alone \\\\\n295\tAny GF (binary)        & $+0.034$ & $<0.01$ & Significant \\\\\n296\tEarly $\\times$ Late    & $-0.003$ & 0.85    & No interaction \\\\\n297\t\\bottomrule\n298\t\\end{tabular}\n299\t\\end{table}\n300\t\n301\tAll four cells match the binary protection hypothesis. Neither early-only nor late-only gradient flow is individually significant ($p = 0.19, 0.24$), but the binary contrast---\\emph{any} gradient flow vs.\\ none---is highly significant ($p < 0.01$, effect $= +3.4$\\,pp). The interaction term is negligible ($-0.003$, $p = 0.85$), confirming additivity: early and late gradient flow contribute independently and interchangeably.\n302\t\n303\tThis rules out a critical-window hypothesis (only early gradient flow matters) and a sustained-supervision hypothesis (continuous gradient flow is required). Gradient flow at \\emph{any} phase of training provides full protection.\n304\t\n305\t%--------------------------------------------------\n306\t\\subsection{BatchNorm Confound Control}\n307\t\\label{sec:exp-batchnorm}\n308\t%--------------------------------------------------\n309\t\n310\tThe occupancy head uses BatchNorm2d, whose running statistics in Detach mode are computed on detached features. Could these statistics create an artifact that explains Detach degradation? We replace BatchNorm2d with LayerNorm in the Detach condition.\n311\t\n312\t\\begin{table}[h]\n313\t\\centering\n314\t\\caption{Detach normalization ablation: BatchNorm vs.\\ LayerNorm.}\n315\t\\label{tab:bn}\n316\t\\begin{tabular}{llccl}\n317\t\\toprule\n318\tCondition & Norm Type & CAR & TSR & vs Vanilla \\\\\n319\t\\midrule\n320\tDetach + BN (original) & BatchNorm2d & $0.746 \\pm 0.002$ & $0.812 \\pm 0.163$ & $-3.4$\\,pp \\\\\n321\tDetach + LN            & LayerNorm   & $0.748$           & $0.830$           & $-3.2$\\,pp \\\\\n322\tVanilla                & N/A         & $0.780 \\pm 0.012$ & $0.907 \\pm 0.009$ & baseline \\\\\n323\tFull OCC + BN          & BatchNorm2d & $0.797 \\pm 0.032$ & $0.894 \\pm 0.050$ & $+1.7$\\,pp \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\tDetach + LayerNorm (CAR $= 0.748$) is statistically indistinguishable from Detach + BatchNorm (CAR $= 0.746$, $\\Delta = 0.2$\\,pp, within Detach seed variance $\\sigma = 0.002$). This eliminates BatchNorm running statistics as a confounding explanation for Detach degradation. The causal factor is gradient flow absence, not architectural details of the auxiliary head.\n329\t\n330\t%--------------------------------------------------\n331\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication: LIBERO-Object}\n332\t\\label{sec:exp-object}\n333\t%--------------------------------------------------\n334\t\n335\tTo test whether our findings generalize beyond a single task distribution, we replicate the core factorial ablation on LIBERO-Object, which emphasizes object manipulation rather than spatial navigation.\n336\t\n337\t\\begin{table}[h]\n338\t\\centering\n339\t\\caption{LIBERO-Object replication (3 seeds, 50K steps, 50 rollouts per task).}\n340\t\\label{tab:object}\n341\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n342\t\\toprule\n343\tCondition & TSR & CAR & vs Vanilla CAR & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n344\t\\midrule\n345\tFull OCC   & $0.915 \\pm 0.040$ & $0.825 \\pm 0.025$ & $+1.5$\\,pp & 0.6 \\\\\n346\tRandom OCC & $0.895 \\pm 0.030$ & $0.815 \\pm 0.028$ & $+0.5$\\,pp & 0.2 \\\\\n347\tVanilla    & $0.920 \\pm 0.012$ & $0.810 \\pm 0.015$ & baseline   & --- \\\\\n348\tDetach     & $0.845 \\pm 0.120$ & $0.775 \\pm 0.008$ & $-3.5$\\,pp & 3.2 \\\\\n349\t\\bottomrule\n350\t\\end{tabular}\n351\t\\end{table}\n352\t\n353\t\\begin{table}[h]\n354\t\\centering\n355\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark pattern consistency.}\n356\t\\label{tab:cross}\n357\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n358\t\\toprule\n359\tPattern & LIBERO-Spatial & LIBERO-Object & Consistent? \\\\\n360\t\\midrule\n361\tDetach $<$ Vanilla       & $-3.4$\\,pp ($d$$=$$3.9$) & $-3.5$\\,pp ($d$$=$$3.2$) & Yes \\\\\n362\tFull OCC $\\approx$ Vanilla & $+1.7$\\,pp             & $+1.5$\\,pp               & Yes \\\\\n363\tRandom $\\approx$ Full OCC  & $-0.8$\\,pp             & $-1.0$\\,pp               & Yes \\\\\n364\tDetach TSR instability     & $\\sigma$$=$$0.163$     & $\\sigma$$=$$0.120$       & Yes \\\\\n365\t\\bottomrule\n366\t\\end{tabular}\n367\t\\end{table}\n368\t\n369\tAll four core patterns replicate on LIBERO-Object: (1) Detach degrades CAR by $-3.5$\\,pp with large effect size (Cohen's $d = 3.2$); (2) Full OCC matches Vanilla within seed variance; (3) Random OCC $\\approx$ Full OCC (1.0\\,pp gap); (4) Detach exhibits high TSR instability ($\\sigma = 0.120$). The consistency across benchmarks with different task distributions strengthens the conclusion that gradient flow protection is a general property of VLA fine-tuning, not an artifact of a specific task suite.\n370\t\n371\t%--------------------------------------------------\n372\t\\section{Discussion}\n373\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n374\t%--------------------------------------------------\n375\t\n376\t\\paragraph{Three-level causal chain.}\n377\tOur experiments establish a mechanistic causal chain: (1) Auxiliary gradient flow aligns action and auxiliary gradients in the encoder's spatial layers (gradient cosine $0.24$ vs.\\ $0.01$); (2) this alignment stabilizes encoder representations during fine-tuning (CKA $0.95$ vs.\\ $0.80$ relative to vanilla); (3) representation stability preserves spatial reasoning capabilities, maintaining collision avoidance performance. Each level of the chain is independently measurable and consistently distinguishes gradient-flow from detached conditions.\n378\t\n379\t\\paragraph{Binary protection.}\n380\tThe dose-response curve's step-function shape and the $2 \\times 2$ temporal design both converge on the same conclusion: gradient flow protection is binary, not graded. Any exposure---early, late, or as brief as 30\\% of training---provides full protection; only complete absence (Detach) causes degradation. This has practical implications: auxiliary tasks can be introduced at any training phase and discontinued early to save compute, with no loss of safety benefit.\n381\t\n382\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n383\tOur analysis is conducted on a single VLA architecture (OpenVLA-scale) with LoRA fine-tuning. While cross-benchmark replication strengthens generality, verification on additional architectures (e.g., diffusion-based policies) and full fine-tuning regimes remains future work. The $n$$=$$3$ seed design provides sufficient power for large effects (Detach, $d > 3$) but may miss smaller effects between gradient-flow conditions.\n384\t\n385\t%--------------------------------------------------\n386\t\\section{Conclusion}\n387\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n388\t%--------------------------------------------------\n389\t\n390\tWe present a mechanistic analysis of how auxiliary spatial supervision affects safety in VLA fine-tuning. Through factorial ablations, gradient analysis, representation probing, and dose-response experiments across two benchmarks, we establish that \\textbf{gradient flow}---not spatial label content---is the necessary and sufficient condition for maintaining collision avoidance performance. Detaching auxiliary gradients actively harms the model ($-3.4$\\,pp CAR, Cohen's $d \\approx 3.9$), while even random-label gradient flow provides full protection. The protection mechanism operates as a binary switch: any gradient flow duration $\\geq$15K steps suffices, at any training phase. These findings provide actionable guidance for practitioners: when adding auxiliary heads to VLAs, preserving gradient flow to the encoder is essential. An auxiliary head with detached gradients is worse than no auxiliary head at all.\n391\t\n392\t\\end{document}\n393\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tiuv7BknzVCPhTpc13ZXwG","session_id":"6fd64fbd-040e-43dd-8257-d68950227536","uuid":"96dff898-0226-4775-8c15-fc54162d1c28","timestamp":"2026-05-23T04:29:50.082Z"}
