{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KYR58814QhBgY1xMcnorXt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n4\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n12\t\\usepackage{authblk}\n13\t\\usepackage{caption}\n14\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n15\t\n16\t\\title{Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make Vision-Language-Action Models Safer?\\\\ A Mechanism Analysis}\n17\t\n18\t\\author[1]{Anonymous Authors}\n19\t\\affil[1]{Anonymous Institution}\n20\t\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t%--------------------------------------------------\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\t%--------------------------------------------------\n30\tVision-language-action (VLA) models fine-tuned for robotic manipulation can lose the spatial awareness encoded in their pretrained vision backbones, leading to unsafe collision-prone behavior. A natural remedy is to add an auxiliary spatial prediction head---such as BEV occupancy---during fine-tuning. But \\emph{why} does this help, and \\emph{what} is the minimal requirement? We present a factorial mechanism analysis across five gradient-flow conditions, four dose-response cutoff points, and two task benchmarks, totalling over 30 training runs. Our central finding is that auxiliary \\textbf{gradient flow} through the vision encoder is the necessary and sufficient condition for maintaining collision avoidance performance, while the \\emph{spatial content} of the auxiliary labels is secondary. Detaching the auxiliary head from the encoder degrades collision avoidance rate (CAR) by $-3.4$\\,pp on LIBERO-Spatial and $-3.5$\\,pp on LIBERO-Object (Cohen's $d > 3$), whereas random-label supervision with intact gradient flow matches the vanilla baseline. Mechanistically, gradient flow aligns action and auxiliary gradients in the encoder's spatial layers (cosine similarity $0.24$ vs.\\ $0.01$ under detachment), stabilizing encoder representations (CKA $0.95$ vs.\\ $0.80$ relative to vanilla). Dose-response analysis reveals binary protection: as few as 15K steps (30\\% of training) suffice, with a step-function transition to degradation only at zero gradient flow. These results establish gradient flow as the causal mechanism and provide practical guidance: if attaching an auxiliary head to a VLA, preserving gradient flow is essential; omitting it actively harms safety.\n31\t\\end{abstract}\n32\t\n33\t%--------------------------------------------------\n34\t\\section{Introduction}\n35\t\\label{sec:intro}\n36\t%--------------------------------------------------\n37\t\n38\tLarge vision-language-action (VLA) models such as OpenVLA leverage pretrained vision encoders (e.g., DINOv2, SigLIP) that encode rich spatial representations. During task-specific fine-tuning, however, these representations can degrade as the encoder adapts to action prediction objectives, potentially compromising safety-relevant spatial awareness such as obstacle detection and collision avoidance.\n39\t\n40\tAuxiliary spatial supervision---predicting bird's-eye-view (BEV) occupancy grids or depth maps alongside action outputs---has been proposed as a mechanism to preserve spatial awareness during fine-tuning. Prior work has shown empirical benefits, but the \\emph{causal mechanism} remains unclear. Is it the spatial content of the labels that provides useful inductive bias? Or is it simply the presence of additional gradient signal flowing through the encoder?\n41\t\n42\tWe address this question through a systematic factorial ablation study on a VLA fine-tuned with LoRA for robotic manipulation. Our experimental design isolates the effect of gradient flow from label content through five conditions:\n43\t\\begin{itemize}\n44\t    \\item \\textbf{Full OCC}: BEV occupancy head with gradient flow to the encoder.\n45\t    \\item \\textbf{Detach}: Identical occupancy head, but gradients are detached (stopped) before entering the encoder.\n46\t    \\item \\textbf{Random OCC}: Occupancy head with random labels but intact gradient flow.\n47\t    \\item \\textbf{Vanilla}: No auxiliary head (action-only fine-tuning baseline).\n48\t    \\item \\textbf{Detach + LayerNorm}: Detach variant controlling for BatchNorm artifacts.\n49\t\\end{itemize}\n50\t\n51\tOur contributions are:\n52\t\\begin{enumerate}\n53\t    \\item \\textbf{Gradient flow is necessary}: Detaching auxiliary gradients degrades CAR by $-3.4$\\,pp (Cohen's $d \\approx 3.9$), while all gradient-flow conditions match or exceed the vanilla baseline.\n54\t    \\item \\textbf{Mechanistic explanation}: We provide a three-level causal chain---gradient alignment (cosine similarity), representation stability (CKA), and behavioral outcome (CAR)---explaining \\emph{why} gradient flow is protective.\n55\t    \\item \\textbf{Practical guidance}: Gradient flow protection is binary (a step function, not dose-dependent), activatable at any training phase, and replicates across benchmarks.\n56\t\\end{enumerate}\n57\t\n58\t%--------------------------------------------------\n59\t\\section{Related Work}\n60\t\\label{sec:related}\n61\t%--------------------------------------------------\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}\n64\tVLA models integrate pretrained vision-language backbones with action prediction heads for robotic control. OpenVLA, RT-2, and $\\pi_0$ represent this paradigm. Fine-tuning with parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA enables task adaptation while partially preserving pretrained representations, but the degree of representation preservation---particularly for safety-relevant spatial features---has not been systematically studied.\n65\t\n66\t\\paragraph{Auxiliary Tasks in Robot Learning.}\n67\tAuxiliary self-supervised objectives have a long history in reinforcement learning and robot learning. Depth prediction, contrastive learning, and reconstruction objectives have been used to improve sample efficiency and representation quality. BEV occupancy prediction has been explored in autonomous driving but remains underexplored for manipulation safety. Critically, prior work evaluates auxiliary tasks by their end-task performance, without isolating whether benefits derive from \\emph{label content} or \\emph{gradient flow}.\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Safety in Learned Robot Policies.}\n70\tSafety-aware robot learning spans control-theoretic approaches (control barrier functions, reachability analysis), constrained optimization, and learned safety critics. These methods typically operate at inference time with explicit safety constraints. Our work is complementary: we study how \\emph{training-time} auxiliary supervision affects the implicit safety properties of a pretrained-then-fine-tuned VLA, with zero additional inference cost.\n71\t\n72\t%--------------------------------------------------\n73\t\\section{Method}\n74\t\\label{sec:method}\n75\t%--------------------------------------------------\n76\t\n77\t\\subsection{Architecture}\n78\t\n79\tWe build on a VLA architecture comprising a DINOv2 spatial encoder (1024-dim) and a SigLIP semantic encoder (1152-dim), whose features are concatenated (2176-dim) and fed to an autoregressive action head. Fine-tuning uses LoRA (rank$=$32, $\\alpha$$=$16, scaling$=$0.5) over 50K steps with learning rate $5 \\times 10^{-4}$.\n80\t\n81\t\\subsection{Auxiliary Occupancy Head}\n82\t\n83\tThe occupancy head takes the concatenated encoder features and predicts a $64 \\times 64$ BEV occupancy grid:\n84\t\\begin{equation}\n85\t    \\hat{O} = \\texttt{OccHead}\\bigl(\\texttt{concat}(f_{\\text{DINOv2}}, f_{\\text{SigLIP}})\\bigr)\n86\t\\end{equation}\n87\tThe head consists of Linear$(2176 \\to 256)$, reshape to $(B, 256, 16, 16)$, two transposed convolutions upsampling to $64 \\times 64$, with BatchNorm2d and GELU activations. Total parameters: 1.2M (0.016\\% of the 7.65B model). Training uses focal loss ($\\alpha$$=$0.25, $\\gamma$$=$2.0) with exponentially decaying weight $w(t) = 0.01 \\cdot \\exp(-t/50000)$.\n88\t\n89\t\\subsection{Experimental Conditions}\n90\t\n91\tThe total loss under the Full OCC condition is:\n92\t\\begin{equation}\n93\t    \\mathcal{L} = \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{action}} + w(t) \\cdot \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{occ}}(\\hat{O}, O^*)\n94\t\\end{equation}\n95\t\n96\tThe five conditions differ in how $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{occ}}$ interacts with the encoder:\n97\t\n98\t\\begin{itemize}\n99\t    \\item \\textbf{Full OCC}: $\\nabla_\\theta \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{occ}}$ flows through the encoder parameters $\\theta$.\n100\t    \\item \\textbf{Detach}: $\\hat{O} = \\texttt{OccHead}(\\texttt{sg}[\\texttt{concat}(f_{\\text{DINOv2}}, f_{\\text{SigLIP}})])$ where $\\texttt{sg}[\\cdot]$ denotes stop-gradient. The occ head trains on frozen features; no gradient reaches the encoder."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VGfo15bnabCdVA3evbQgKc","session_id":"6fd64fbd-040e-43dd-8257-d68950227536","uuid":"b298b724-b1d3-4aba-89c2-4294e04a80f1","timestamp":"2026-05-23T04:29:52.388Z"}
