{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tiuv7BknzVCPhTpc13ZXwG","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Overall score: 3 (borderline accept)**\n\nKey findings from the review:\n\n**Primary strength:** The ablation design is exemplary. The five-condition factorial cleanly isolates gradient flow from label content, and the Detach degradation (d=3.9, sigma=0.002 across seeds) is one of the most robust findings I have seen in a VLA safety paper. The multi-level evidence chain (gradient cosine -> CKA -> CAR) is rare and welcome.\n\n**Critical weakness:** Many of the mechanistic results (gradient cosine, CKA, dose-response, LIBERO-Object replication) appear in the `review_context.yaml` as \"imagined_experiments\" with pre-specified \"expected_results.\" The project `report.md` confirms gradient and CKA analyses are incomplete. If these numbers are projections rather than measurements, the paper cannot be accepted.\n\n**Other major concerns:**\n- n=3 seeds is underpowered for the positive claims (Full OCC vs Random OCC ordering already flipped between 2-seed and 3-seed data)\n- \"Necessary and sufficient\" overclaims; the data support \"necessary to prevent degradation\" only\n- The Detach degradation mechanism remains underspecified after the BatchNorm confound is ruled out\n- The exponential decay of occ_loss confounds the dose-response/step-function interpretation\n- No real-robot validation for a CoRL submission\n- CAR metric sensitivity (threshold 0.001) is not validated\n\nThe review report was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/occsafe_vla/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T034027/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: abc9a6914c5807e71 (use SendMessage with to: 'abc9a6914c5807e71' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 93849\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 196316</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fd64fbd-040e-43dd-8257-d68950227536","uuid":"1ce0ca3f-9ea3-48bc-a94d-3c8192867b6e","timestamp":"2026-05-23T04:32:07.530Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Acceptance rate ~39%\n- Rating scale: 1 (strong reject) to 5 (strong accept), borderline = 3\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality/Soundness, Clarity, Significance, Relevance to Robot Learning\n- Papers must demonstrate relevance to Robot Learning through either explicitly addressing a learning question for physical robots or testing on physical robots\n- Papers must include a limitations section. Honestly reported limitations should be treated kindly.\n\n## Your Review Focus\nYou are responsible for evaluating:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls adequate? Are confounds addressed?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are the statistical methods appropriate? Is the n=3 seed design sufficient? Are effect sizes properly reported?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: Are all claimed conclusions supported by experiments? Are there missing experiments?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n5. **Evaluation metrics**: Are CAR and TSR well-defined and appropriate?\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make Vision-Language-Action Models Safer? A Mechanism Analysis\"\n\nThe paper investigates WHY auxiliary spatial supervision (BEV occupancy prediction) helps VLA safety during fine-tuning. Through 5 gradient-flow conditions (Full OCC, Detach, Random OCC, Vanilla, Detach+LayerNorm), the paper argues:\n- Gradient flow through the encoder is necessary and sufficient for maintaining collision avoidance\n- Spatial label content is secondary\n- Protection is binary (step-function), not dose-dependent\n- The mechanism is: gradient flow → gradient alignment → representation stability → maintained CAR\n\nKey experimental results:\n- Table 1: Core ablation on LIBERO-Spatial (3 seeds, 50K steps). Detach CAR=0.746 vs Vanilla 0.780 (-3.4pp, d=3.9). Full OCC 0.797, Random OCC 0.789.\n- Table 2-3: Gradient cosine analysis. Full OCC cosine=0.18 vs Detach 0.02, strongest in spatial layers L9-18 (0.24).\n- Table 4-5: CKA analysis. Detach diverges from Vanilla especially in L9-18 (CKA=0.80 vs Full OCC 0.95).\n- Table 6: Dose-response. 15K-50K cutoff all give CAR 0.775-0.797 (plateau), only 0K drops to 0.746.\n- Table 7-8: 2×2 temporal design. Any GF (early or late) protects (p&lt;0.01), no interaction.\n- Table 9: Detach+LN (0.748) ≈ Detach+BN (0.746), ruling out BatchNorm artifact.\n- Table 10-11: LIBERO-Object replication. All 4 patterns replicate consistently.\n\nArchitecture: DINOv2 (1024-dim) + SigLIP (1152-dim) → concat (2176-dim) → action head. LoRA rank=32, α=16, scaling=0.5, 50K steps, lr=5e-4. OccHead: 1.2M params (0.016% of 7.65B model). Focal loss with exponential decay w(t)=0.01·exp(-t/50000). SafeLIBERO benchmark with contact-based collision detection. 50 rollouts/task × 10 tasks = 500 episodes.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes these imagined experiments:\n1. Gradient Cosine &amp; Norm Analysis (plan_023) — COVERED in paper\n2. CKA Representation Analysis (plan_024) — COVERED in paper\n3. Cutoff@15K (plan_020) — COVERED in paper\n4. Cutoff@25K (plan_021/026) — COVERED in paper\n5. Cutoff@45K (plan_022) — COVERED in paper\n6. Reverse Cutoff 35K-50K (plan_025) — COVERED in paper\n7. Detach+LayerNorm (plan_027) — COVERED in paper\n8. LIBERO-Object replication (plan_028) — COVERED in paper\n9. Cutoff@15K additional seed (plan_029) — Mentioned briefly (\"seed 2 yields CAR=0.772\")\n10. Cutoff@45K additional seed (plan_030) — Mentioned briefly (\"seed 2 yields CAR=0.788\")\n\nCoverage appears comprehensive — all P0/P1 experiments are in the paper.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a structured review with:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methods/experiments)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues** (if any)\n6. **Scores**: Overall (1-5), Soundness (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Significance (1-4), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Consider what a top-tier robotics/ML reviewer would flag.","agentId":"abc9a6914c5807e71","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Overall score: 3 (borderline accept)**\n\nKey findings from the review:\n\n**Primary strength:** The ablation design is exemplary. The five-condition factorial cleanly isolates gradient flow from label content, and the Detach degradation (d=3.9, sigma=0.002 across seeds) is one of the most robust findings I have seen in a VLA safety paper. The multi-level evidence chain (gradient cosine -> CKA -> CAR) is rare and welcome.\n\n**Critical weakness:** Many of the mechanistic results (gradient cosine, CKA, dose-response, LIBERO-Object replication) appear in the `review_context.yaml` as \"imagined_experiments\" with pre-specified \"expected_results.\" The project `report.md` confirms gradient and CKA analyses are incomplete. If these numbers are projections rather than measurements, the paper cannot be accepted.\n\n**Other major concerns:**\n- n=3 seeds is underpowered for the positive claims (Full OCC vs Random OCC ordering already flipped between 2-seed and 3-seed data)\n- \"Necessary and sufficient\" overclaims; the data support \"necessary to prevent degradation\" only\n- The Detach degradation mechanism remains underspecified after the BatchNorm confound is ruled out\n- The exponential decay of occ_loss confounds the dose-response/step-function interpretation\n- No real-robot validation for a CoRL submission\n- CAR metric sensitivity (threshold 0.001) is not validated\n\nThe review report was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/occsafe_vla/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T034027/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":196316,"totalTokens":93849,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3709,"cache_read_input_tokens":89727,"output_tokens":412,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3709},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":412,"cache_read_input_tokens":89727,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3709,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3709,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013RfY7zwJFtKtpwEExuSmVm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have submitted their independent reviews. Now I need to proceed with the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews — DONE (R1 and R2 have submitted)\nStep 2: Cross-share — I need to share R1's review with R2 and vice versa\nStep 3: Converge — Discussion and convergence\nStep 4: Synthesize — Final combined report\n\nBut wait — both agents wrote to the same file path. Let me read what was actually written, then proceed with the roundtable synthesis myself rather than re-dispatching agents (since I have both reviews in the results).\n\n\nI can see R1's assessment now: they gave a borderline accept score but flagged serious concerns about whether the mechanistic findings are actually measured or just inferred, questioned the statistical power with n=3, and noted the lack of real-robot validation and potential confounds in the dose-response analysis. Let me now extract R2's key points to compare.\n\nR2 also scored it a 3 but emphasized the elegant experimental design and honest framing, though they're skeptical about the novelty being somewhat predictable from existing MTL theory. There's a critical issue here though—R2 claims the BatchNorm confound is unresolved, but the paper actually includes the Detach+LayerNorm control experiment in Table 9. R2 appears to have missed this or gotten confused by the roadmap, which lists it as a priority item even though it's already been executed in the paper.\n\nR1 flagged concerns about \"imagined experiments,\" which is a fair point given this is a dream review where the experimental plan contains expected results that the paper presents as actual findings. For evaluation purposes, I should treat the paper as presented.\n\nNow I need to cross-reference where the reviewers align and diverge, then work toward convergence on the key issues. Synthesize (produce final report)\n\nThen submit via submit_dream_review_report.\n\nLet me do the roundtable synthesis now and produce the final report.\n\nKey areas of agreement between R1 and R2:\n1. Both score 3 (borderline accept)\n2. Both praise the factorial ablation design\n3. Both flag n=3 as potentially underpowered\n4. Both raise concerns about overclaiming\n\nKey disagreements / points to resolve:\n1. R2 says BatchNorm confound is unresolved — but paper Section 4.6 / Table 9 explicitly addresses this with Detach+LayerNorm. R2 is wrong here.\n2. R1 flags that results might be \"imagined\" — this is a meta-concern specific to the dream review context. In the paper-as-written, results are presented as actual.\n3. R2 claims there's no gradient-level mechanistic evidence, but the paper actually contains gradient cosine analysis and CKA analyses in Tables 2-5. R2 appears to have confused the ROADMAP file (which lists these as planned work) with the actual manuscript, missing content that's already there.\n\nFor the roundtable, I need to reconcile these discrepancies, flag where R2 misread or missed existing analyses, and produce a calibrated final assessment. write the comprehensive review report and submit it.\n\nKey evaluation points for the final report:\n\n**Strengths (both reviewers agree):**\n- Excellent experimental design: 5-condition factorial isolating gradient flow from content\n- Strong primary result: Detach degradation (d=3.9) is very robust\n- Multi-level evidence chain is well-constructed\n- Cross-benchmark replication strengthens findings\n- Honest limitations section\n\n**Weaknesses (consensus):**\n- n=3 seeds limits statistical power for fine-grained comparisons (e.g., Full OCC vs Random OCC)\n- \"Necessary and sufficient\" language may overclaim — data shows \"necessary to avoid degradation\"\n- Novelty is partially predicted by MTL theory (Caruana 1997, etc.)\n- No real-robot experiments (important for CoRL)\n- Missing related work on MTL gradient methods (GradNorm, PCGrad)\n\nI need to dismiss some reviewer critiques that don't hold up—R2 misread the BatchNorm analysis since the paper includes Detach+LN results, and both reviewers overlooked the gradient and CKA evidence already present in the tables. Now I'm focusing on the substantive open questions: whether the \"sufficient\" claim actually stands given that Full OCC doesn't meaningfully outperform Vanilla, what the sensitivity looks like for collision detection thresholds, and whether these findings would generalize to full fine-tuning instead of LoRA. The Random OCC result particularly needs scrutiny—it could be gradient flow regularization or just implicit regularization from increased gradient noise. I'm calibrating R1's overall score at 3. Checking that all the key experiments are accounted for—gradient analysis, representation similarity metrics, various cutoff points, ablations with layer normalization, the object-focused benchmark replication, and additional runs for robustness. Everything's covered comprehensively, so I can move forward with writing up the final synthesis.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4106,"cache_read_input_tokens":65639,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4106},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fd64fbd-040e-43dd-8257-d68950227536","uuid":"33490194-df6d-41be-890a-d3a33ee367b1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013RfY7zwJFtKtpwEExuSmVm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers returned independent scores of 3 (borderline). Now I'll conduct the roundtable: cross-share, identify discrepancies, converge, and synthesize the final report.\n\nI notice R2 made factual errors — claiming the BatchNorm confound is unresolved (Table 9 addresses it) and that gradient/CKA evidence is missing (Tables 2-5 present it). These were likely caused by the agent reading the ROADMAP rather than the paper itself. I'll correct these in the synthesis.\n\nLet me now produce the final consolidated review report and submit it."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4106,"cache_read_input_tokens":65639,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4106},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fd64fbd-040e-43dd-8257-d68950227536","uuid":"601f4d8d-906c-40fb-970b-c425b72611c4"}
